Autumn 1923. At half past nine they found Amos Reed, the lock-keeper, drowned in his own lock. It looks like a fall from the gate in the dark. You are the county constable. The coroner comes at three. By then you need a case, or it goes down as an accident.

Towpath

A cinder path along the canal. The cottage door is west, the lock north, the lock hut south. Water knocks against the lock gate.

Exits: west, north, south.

> w
w

Kitchen

The cottage kitchen, low and warm, with a black iron stove. The door to the towpath is east, the parlour west, the stairs go up.

A pair of town boots dries by the stove. Hester Reed, Amos's daughter, is here.

Exits: east, west, up.

> examine boots
examine boots

Town boots, wet to the ankle, with canal mud in the welts. Marsh said he never left the parlour.

You write it in your notebook: Marsh was out in the wet.

> up
up

Amos's Room

Amos Reed lies on his bed under a sheet, where they carried him. His coat hangs on the door. Hester's room is west.

The coat contains a pocket notebook.

Exits: down, west.

> examine notebook
examine notebook

Amos's notebook, in a careful hand. The last line: "Ledger p. 41 to 44 wrong. Told M."

You write it in your notebook: the ledger, and "M".

> down
down

Kitchen

The cottage kitchen, low and warm, with a black iron stove. The door to the towpath is east, the parlour west, the stairs go up.

A pair of town boots dries by the stove. Hester Reed, Amos's daughter, is here. Ivo Marsh, the toll clerk, is here.

Exits: east, west, up.

Hester Reed goes east.

Marsh cleans a pair of boots by the stove, quickly, and does not look at you.

> w
w

Parlour

A cold best room. The company's toll ledger lies open on the table under the lamp.

Exits: east.

Ivo Marsh comes in.

> show notes to marsh
show notes to marsh

Marsh reads the page, and his hands go still for the first time tonight. Then he is on his feet, his eyes on the door. In a moment he will be out on the towpath.

> wait
wait

Time passes.

Marsh is out of the door before you can turn. Running feet go east along the towpath, into the dark.

He ran

By dawn Ivo Marsh is on a train to the coast, and nobody stops him. He ran from what he read in your notebook: no weapon, the chance, and a motive, but not the pages.